A||||7||FieroBUZZ||Different wheel size front and rear?||||1||
Z||000000||FieroBUZZ||12-03-2001||11:11 AM||||Chassis Guys:<BR>I've seen 16's, 17's, and even 18's but is there any technical reason for not running a different size front and rear.  I mean an identical wheel in a different size, like a 16 front and 17 rear. <P> I would expect overall tire diameter to be very similar or maybe a very slight rake. It's an idea for a non Fiero front end I'm toying with for one of my SE's.  It would be more 50's style with a grill and headlights in the fenders, although maybe styled along the modern HID type rather than round.<P>Gary||||reg||1
Z||000001||ITB 2M4||12-03-2001||11:46 AM||||Other than it's easier for people to buy 4 tires of the same size at one time, there is no real reason.  Most bikes have different size wheels, to help with turning and steering feedback.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4<BR>AOL IM- DGPFiero||||reg||10
Z||000002||FieroBUZZ||12-03-2001||01:19 PM||||I have 7 complete sets of wheels and tires and only 1 has the same size (205) front and rear.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Gary||||reg||6
Z||000003||Formula88||12-03-2001||02:18 PM||||Even the factory 88 Formulas and GTs used different size rims front and rear.  Same diameter, but different widths.  15x6 front with 205/60-15s and 15x7 rear wtih 215/60-15s.  So, there's no reason why staggering the sizes would hurt.  Fieros actually seem to prefer it.||||reg||3
Z||000004||Pyric||12-03-2001||02:50 PM||||The only reason I can think of is that it lowers your options on how to rotate them.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||artherd||12-03-2001||09:24 PM||||Fieros like staggared sizes.  If not different actual wheel diameters, then lower profile tyres front, and slightly taller rear.<P>Several listers have 16" fronts with 17" rears.<P>Some have 17" front and 18" rear.<P>Go for it, rotating tyres strictly is for non-sports cars.<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000006||Joe Torma||12-03-2001||10:47 PM||||Measure the wheel well diameter.  The rear is 1" larger.  That's why I got 16/17s  I hated the fact that the wheel gap was more in the back.  I may go up to 17/18...dunno.<P>------------------<BR>Its never going to be finished...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig2_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fierobruiser||12-03-2001||11:23 PM||||I bought my 88 Coupe with 205/60-14 fronts and 195/70-14 rears. Since the tires had a lot of tread left, I've been running it that way. I don't notice any ill handling effects and it too has a nice little rake to it. I don't know if the larger rears have any effect but I the speedo seems to read correctly and I get about 30mpg around town with very minimal use of 5th gear.In the spring however, I will have a new set of 205/60-15 Dunlops mounted on my new Baretta lace wheels.I'm also going to remove the factory alloys on the car now and replace them with steel 14" Sunbird wheels with 195/70-14's for the snow and salt this winter. There's not much sidewall flex in the snow and I think that the smaller tire should bite into the snow a little better. <P>------------------<BR>Notchbacks Rule<BR>85GT(show car)<BR>88Coupe (daily driver)<BR>84 Coupe(project car)||||reg||1
